result:
Fixed. The bug was in the abstract-name branch of both `UnixListener::bind` and `UnixStream::connect`:

```rust
let os_str_bytes = path.as_ref().as_os_str().as_bytes();
if os_str_bytes.starts_with(b"\0") {
    StdSocketAddr::from_abstract_name(os_str_bytes)?   // <-- NUL passed through
```

`std`'s `SocketAddr::from_abstract_name` takes the *bare* name and prepends the leading NUL itself. Passing the full path `"\0aaa"` therefore registered `\0\0aaa`, and since abstract names are fixed-length within `sun_path`, the extra byte also pushed the last character out — `as_abstract_name()` came back as `[0, 97, 97]` instead of `[97, 97, 97]`.

The fix (`tokio/src/net/unix/listener.rs:83`, `tokio/src/net/unix/stream.rs:79`) strips the prefix before handing the name over:

```rust
// `from_abstract_name` prepends the leading null byte itself, so it
// must be stripped from the name first to avoid binding a name with
// an extra leading null byte.
if let Some(name) = os_str_bytes.strip_prefix(b"\0") {
    StdSocketAddr::from_abstract_name(name)?
} else {
    StdSocketAddr::from_pathname(path)?
}
```

Using `strip_prefix` also collapses the check and the trim into one step, so the two can't drift apart.

Verification:
- `abstract_socket_name` fails before the change (`left: [0, 97, 97]`, `right: [97, 97, 97]`) and passes after.
- Full `uds_stream` suite (8 tests) plus `uds_datagram`, `uds_socket`, `uds_split`, `uds_cred` all pass.

I checked `UnixSocket::bind`/`connect` in `tokio/src/net/unix/socket.rs` as well — they route through `socket2::SockAddr::unix`, which already handles the abstract case correctly (copies the bytes verbatim and omits the trailing NUL from the length), so they needed no change. `cargo clippy` isn't installed in this environment, so I couldn't lint.
